3. SCSS File Organization
Current state
The file is already broken into banner-commented sections (// ── Section Name ──), but the sections are ordered roughly in the chronological order features were added to the decks, not by logical category. Generic, broadly-reusable utilities (.emphasis, .muted, .columns-equal, .rotate-*) are interleaved with styles that exist for exactly one specific slide (the memory-model slide, the “how many 5s” exercise, the normative-conformity phonetic-spelling slide).
Proposed section order
/*-- scss:defaults --*/
// 1. TOKENS — fonts, colors, heading scale, spacing (already isolated, keep as-is: lines 1–36)

/*-- scss:rules --*/

// 2. CSS CUSTOM PROPERTY BRIDGE
//    Exposes brand tokens as --variables for use in .qmd attributes (lines 42–51)

// 3. BASE / REVEAL OVERRIDES
//    Generic tweaks to reveal.js's own built-in structures (divider slides,
//    dark-background text color, footer, logo, list spacing, code block defaults)
//    — current lines 53–66, 495–524, 471–493

// 4. LAYOUT UTILITIES
//    Reusable, content-agnostic structural helpers usable on any slide:
//    .columns / .column-right / .columns-equal, section.center,
//    .slide-stack + :has(.slide-stack), .no-padding, .text-panel-{side}-{width},
//    .rotate-ccw-15 / .rotate-cw-15
//    — current lines 68–76, 131–168, 147–159, 526–532, 542–545, 571–579

// 5. TYPOGRAPHY UTILITIES
//    Reusable text treatments not tied to one slide's content:
//    .emphasis, .muted, .body-light, blockquote + .quote-attribution,
//    .hollow-square-bullets, .highlight-line
//    — current lines 185–196, 277–279, 446–460, 490–493, 547–569

// 6. REUSABLE COMPONENTS
//    Named, multi-slide components: title slide (.title-custom, .title-text,
//    .subtitle-text, .title-bird, .subtitle-slide-text), table family
//    (.raw-table-demo, .kable-table*, .table-system-font, gt svg fix),
//    code sizing (.code-size-1/2/3), callouts (.callout-oval/rect/line-vertical/label),
//    .slide-source-footer, slide-body-{med,lg}-text[-emphasis]
//    — current lines 78–129, 354–415, 471–493, 330–352, 511–518, 417–443

// 7. PER-SLIDE / ONE-OFF OVERRIDES
//    Single-use styles tied to one specific slide's exact content — grouped
//    by which slide/talk they belong to:
//      - Memory-model slide: .memory-label, .memory-detail(-*)
//      - "How many 5s" exercise: .digit-wall, .fragment.custom.five-pop, .blink
//      - Data-pixel-ratio slide: .ratio-fraction(-lg), .ratio-annotation, .no-logo
//      - Normative-conformity talk: .norm-conform, .norm-speech, .norm-advice, .center-col
//    — current lines 198–273, 275–328, 581–619
This groups by “how reusable/generic is this” (tokens → base → layout → typography → components → one-offs), which matches the order you described wanting. The .callout-tip “Highlight box utility” (styles.scss:462–469) would land in section 6 if kept, or be deleted per the naming-collision note above.
Specificity conflicts — do not reorder these relative to each other without flagging it
Per your instructions, here are the selector pairs/groups with equal CSS specificity that set overlapping properties. Reordering any of these changes which declaration wins if both ever match the same element:
A) Three “vertical-centering” selectors, all specificity (0,3,1):



Selector
Lines
Properties set




.reveal .slides section.title-custom
80–85
display: flex, flex-direction: column, justify-content: center, height: 100%


.reveal .slides section:has(.slide-stack)
132–136
display: flex !important, flex-direction: column, height: 100%


.reveal .slides section.statement
171–176
display: flex, flex-direction: column, justify-content: center, height: 100%



All three compute to specificity (0,3,1) — .reveal, .slides, and one more class each contribute one class-level point (:has(.slide-stack)’s specificity is that of its argument, .slide-stack, per spec), and section is the one element. Currently no slide in the project applies more than one of these three conditions at once (.statement is in fact unused in any .qmd right now — see below), so there’s no live conflict, but if a future slide combines .title-custom or .statement with a nested .slide-stack, or if .statement gets adopted on a slide containing .slide-stack, source order decides the shared properties (except display, which :has(.slide-stack) pins via !important regardless of order).
B) Table border rules, both specificity (0,2,1) at the th/td level:



Selector
Lines
Property




.reveal .kable-table-borders th, td
391–393
border: 1px solid $body-color (shorthand — touches all four sides)


.reveal .kable-table-light th, td
404–407
border-bottom: 1px solid $muted



Currently mutually exclusive by usage — every table in _05-example-table.qmd applies exactly one of kable-table-borders or kable-table-light, never both. But they’re the same specificity and both touch the bottom border edge, so if ever combined on one table, order (and shorthand-vs-longhand interaction) determines the result.
C) Lower priority — same-specificity size variants, currently non-overlapping by usage:



Selector
Lines




.ratio-fraction
282–298


.ratio-fraction-lg
307–323



Both (0,1,0) at the base class, sharing color/text-align/font-weight/font-size/width. These are clearly designed as alternate size variants for different slides (confirmed: used on two different slides in _03-data-pixel-ratio.qmd, never together), so this is low-risk — flagging only because it technically meets your literal criteria.
Human Feedback

Implement the proposed section order. Include a clear heading (not numbered) in all caps for each major section to match the proposal here. Add a brief commented description underneath each of those all caps titles to lightly elaborate on that grouping criteria.
Remove .statement entirely from the .scss, since it was not used.
Because there are no current overlaps identified for any of the specificity matches, go ahead and re-order those logically and into the appropriate category
